<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Light</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Colonel William Light</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Light was a British army officer and surveyor, the first Surveyor-General of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>, the son of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-light">Francis Light</a>, he served in the Peninsular War before sailing to South Australia in 1836. He chose the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> and laid out its grid of streets and parklands, despite criticism from Governor Hindmarsh, and died in 1839.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-light.jpg" alt="William Light" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Light · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Light.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Light was born on 27 April 1786, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-light">Francis Light</a>, the founder and superintendent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>, and Martinha Rozells, who was of Portuguese or French, and Siamese or Malay descent. He was legally classed as Eurasian, a designation that placed him between the natives and the Europeans. In 1799, aged 13, he volunteered for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> and left two years later as a midshipman. Interned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> broke out in May 1803, he escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> on 5 January 1804 and spent the next years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 5 May 1808 Light bought a cornet's commission in the 4th (Queen's Own) Regiment of Dragoons and was promoted lieutenant in April 1809 on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. In 1824 he married Mary Bennet, an illegitimate daughter of the 3rd Duke of Richmond. The couple lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he published Views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> in 1828, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1830 he first met promoters of a new colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-james-napier">Charles James Napier</a> recommended him as its governor, but the post had been promised to Hindmarsh, and on 4 February 1836 Light was appointed Surveyor-General instead.</p><p>He was the first to chart the Port Adelaide River accurately, and he chose and laid out the city in eight weeks. The site spanned the River Torrens, known to the local people as Karra Wirra Parri; he expected the Adelaide Hills to bring higher rainfall to the plain, and sites on Encounter Bay, Kangaroo Island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holdfast-bay">Holdfast Bay</a> had been rejected. His plan ringed the city with 2,332 acres of park, 32 of them reserved for what is now West Terrace Cemetery. On 21 June 1838 he resigned rather than survey 150 square miles in a week by less accurate methods, and formed the firm Light, Finniss &amp; Co.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Light suffered from tuberculosis and was nursed by Maria Gandy, named in his will as his housekeeper but thought to be his de facto wife; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> society shunned the couple, and the Reverend Charles Beaumont Howard refused to visit him. A fire in January 1839 destroyed the Land and Survey Office and his hut, with many of his paintings. He died in Adelaide on 6 October 1839, and more than 3,000 people attended his burial in Light Square, where he remains the only person legally buried within the city's square mile. The statue by William Birnie Rhind, unveiled in Victoria Square on 27 November 1906, was moved to Montefiore Hill in 1938 and renamed Light's Vision.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Light” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Colonel William Light</b> chose and laid out the site of Adelaide.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-south-australia">Province of South Australia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kuala Kedah, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.1,100.3&amp;year=1820" aria-label="Kuala Kedah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7329 1936 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Light"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7779 2206h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7779 2206h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.1,100.3&amp;year=1820">What was here in 1820? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
